    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 4,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 538.867, 540.005, 565.28 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Table 1: Physical parameters and numerical values of the three-element Windkessel parameters for every outlet."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 5,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 495.397, 539.996, 521.81 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "a semi-implicit 3D-0D coupling scheme as seen in [9]. The full algorithm is detailed in the appendix."
  }, {
    "type" : "heading",
    "pdfua_tag" : "H1",
    "id" : 6,
    "level" : "Doctitle",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 464.578, 256.307, 476.545 ],
    "heading level" : 1,
    "font" : "CMBX12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "3.1.2 Synthetic measurements"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 7,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 384.628, 540.006, 454.379 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "The forward solution is generated with a time step of dt = 1ms and undersampled in time to dtmeas = 15ms, leading to a total of 56 measurements. From the solution of the forward problem, we simulate a PC-MRI acquisition by subsampling into a rectangular measurement mesh with a resolution of [2mm,2mm,2mm] and then applying the process described in Section 2.1 with a venc of double the maximal velocity. The magnitude is modelled as"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 8,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 179.925, 350.843, 221.911, 362.81 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "M(x) ="
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 9,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 234.859, 341.697, 419.176, 370.999 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "1.0 if x is in the lumen of the vessel 0.5 otherwise."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 10,
    "level" : "3",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 519.189, 350.843, 540.001, 362.81 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "(16)"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 11,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 301.129, 540.003, 329.574 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Finally a complex Gaussian noise ϵ ∈ CN is added with a signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) of 15. Fifty independent realizations of the noise were generated."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 12,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 200.008, 540.005, 298.65 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "For comparison, we reconstructed velocity measurements from these synthetic measurements using the Berkeley Advanced Reconstruction Toolbox (BART)[11]. BART is a command-line-based software that provides a flexible framework of compressed sensing methods, as well as tools for simulation, pre-processing, and image reconstruction, providing a multitude of different regularization options. In this work, we have used this toolbox for compressed sensing reconstructions of the velocities, using total variation in time as for the regularization."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 13,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 153.556, 539.997, 197.529 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "Next, the sampling mask is applied to these simulated frequency space measurements. We take a 2D subsampled mask in the x − y-plane and sample fully in the z-direction as in [4]. We consider different subsampling rates R = NNsampled"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 16,
    "level" : "4",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 72.0, 98.887, 540.004, 168.637 ],
    "font" : "CMR12",
    "font size" : 11.955,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "= 8,16,32, with two different masks: the pseudo-spiral mask and the pseudo-random Gaussian mask, which is sampled according to a Gaussian probability distribution, as shown in Figure 2. For the pseudo-spiral mask, the points are placed evenly on a cartesian grid along a spiral with six turns and a final radius reaching the edge of the mask."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 15,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 359.352, 152.649, 375.148, 158.626 ],
